  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Multisectoral Emergency Response to Conflict and Climate Shock Affected Internally Displaced Populations and Communities in Northern Mozambique &#x2013; Phase II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mozambique faces a triple humanitarian crisis with a conflict, reoccurring disasters related to natural hazards due to climate shocks, and the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic have become incredibly complex. For over four years, the province of Cabo Delgado in Mozambique has experienced brutal attacks by armed groups, while neighboring provinces of Nampula, Niassa, Zambezia and Sofala are seeing a spillover of displacement. Moreover, consecutive disasters related to natural hazards owing to Mozambique being situated in a cyclone prone region, have caused widespread destruction of infrastructure and displacement in the last months (Tropical Storm Ana in January 2022, Tropical Cyclone Gombe in March 2022).Through this project, IOM is implementing a large multi-sector emergency response programme in line with the 2022 Humanitarian Response Plan for Mozambique, that operates across six sectors, namely Camp Coordination and Camp Management, Shelter and Non-Food Items, Emergency Health, Protection and Mental Health and Psychosocial Support, Displacement Tracking, and Water, Sanitation and Hygiene. In addition, IOM has also made commitments on Prevention of Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (PSEA) that include inter-agency coordination to prevent and address SEA incidents, including active participation in in-country PSEA Networks.</narrative>
  </description>
